        {"param": "k", "low": 2, "high": 4, "choices": null}
      ],
      "budget_share": 0.2,
      "why": "k chained rotations/cycle with a shared FSM can reach 50 MSPS if Fmax x k >= 50 (i.e. Fmax >= 12.5-25 MHz), which is much easier than 50 MHz. It uses fewer registers than pipelined (lower power) but a wider combinational path. Worth a smaller share to confirm whether it lands on the front; if its Fmax is too low it will be pruned, but it is a plausible low-power candidate."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "The hard constraint is throughput >= 50 MSPS. iterative (1 result per N+3 cycles) would need Fmax >= ~50*(N+3) MHz, which is implausible, so it is excluded. The viable families are pipelined, pipelined_m (1 result/cycle, need Fmax>=50 MHz) and unrolled_k (k results/cycle, need Fmax>=50/k MHz). Accuracy (max_abs_err <= 2^-12) depends only on W, N, guards, rounding and is identical across families, so I bound W=14-20 and N=12-18 with small guards to sit just above the accuracy floor and let the optimizer trade extra bits (accuracy_bits) against power. The budget is weighted toward pipelined (0.45) and pipelined_m (0.35) because they are the most likely to satisfy Fmax>=50 MHz and define the power/accuracy front, with unrolled_k (0.2) as a lower-Fmax, lower-register alternative. Rounding is left to both choices since round can improve accuracy_bits at a small area/power cost, directly shaping the trade-off curve the hypervolume rewards."
}

